            <ArticleTitle>Impact of pain on recreational activities of nursing home residents.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>44-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The high prevalence of pain in older adults in nursing homes and long-term care institutions is a challenging problem. Nursing home residents were questioned to evaluate whether pain might be an obstacle to their active participation in recreational activities, especially physical activities. This study shows that the intensity and daily experience of pain are both obstacles to participation in physical activities, especially in the oldest patients. The findings also show that participation is linked to the autonomy of the patient; more information about the benefits of physical activity, and coordinating efforts in this area are needed in order to help the elderly preserve their autonomy and quality of life.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
